1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,038
A very big volume came in.

2
00:00:07,488 --> 00:00:09,838
Buyers pushed with everything they had.

3
00:00:10,289 --> 00:00:13,293
Yet price did not go up a single point.

4
00:00:13,743 --> 00:00:16,329
Instead, a little later it fell.

5
00:00:16,779 --> 00:00:20,984
So much effort, and zero result? How?

6
00:00:21,433 --> 00:00:25,222
To understand it, you need to know two kinds of traders.

7
00:00:26,222 --> 00:00:31,055
Hello, this is episode five of the SPIDER Order Flow series.

8
00:00:31,505 --> 00:00:38,976
Last time we saw: market orders eat what sits in the book, and that is what moves price.

9
00:00:39,426 --> 00:00:49,953
Three questions today. Who are aggressive and passive traders? Who moves price and who stops it? And what is effort versus result?

10
00:00:50,953 --> 00:00:53,304
In the market there are two kinds of people.

11
00:00:53,754 --> 00:00:59,162
The first buyer is in a hurry. He wants it now, at whatever the going price is.

12
00:00:59,612 --> 00:01:03,896
This is the aggressive trader. His tool is the market order.

13
00:01:04,346 --> 00:01:10,771
The second man sits at his stall. He has written his price and is waiting.

14
00:01:11,222 --> 00:01:15,271
This is the passive trader. His tool is the limit order.

15
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:20,710
The aggressive one gets filled instantly, but pays the cost of the spread.

16
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:26,646
The passive one gets a better price, but there is no guarantee the trade will happen.

17
00:01:27,646 --> 00:01:33,759
They have other names too. The aggressive one is a taker, because he takes from the book.

18
00:01:34,208 --> 00:01:39,538
The passive one is a maker, because he makes the market by placing orders in the book.

19
00:01:39,988 --> 00:01:46,518
Whatever the name, the idea is the same: one takes, the other places.

20
00:01:47,518 --> 00:01:51,071
Now the real question: who moves the price?

21
00:01:51,521 --> 00:01:56,928
A passive order cannot move price by itself. It just sits where it is.

22
00:01:57,378 --> 00:02:03,256
No matter how much sits in the book, until someone takes it, price stays put.

23
00:02:03,705 --> 00:02:09,270
Price moves only when an aggressive order arrives and eats what is in its path.

24
00:02:09,720 --> 00:02:13,926
So rule one: the aggressive trader moves the price.

25
00:02:14,926 --> 00:02:18,477
But the aggressive side does not decide everything alone.

26
00:02:18,928 --> 00:02:24,805
How much passive supply sits in front of it decides how far price will move.

27
00:02:25,256 --> 00:02:30,245
If little is in the way, even a small push carries price far.

28
00:02:30,695 --> 00:02:36,808
If a very large passive order is in the way, price stalls there even after a full push.

29
00:02:37,257 --> 00:02:45,303
So rule two: aggressive pushes, passive stops. Price is the result of their fight.

30
00:02:46,303 --> 00:02:48,890
This fight can end in three ways.

31
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:56,654
One: aggressive buyers were stronger. Passive sellers were few. Price broke higher.

32
00:02:57,103 --> 00:03:07,553
Two: aggressive buyers came, but passive sellers absorbed all of it. Price stalled. This is called absorption, and it gets its own episode later.

33
00:03:08,003 --> 00:03:14,924
Three: aggressive buyers got tired, the push ran out. Price came back down.

34
00:03:15,375 --> 00:03:20,521
On a chart all three look different, but the question is the same: who was stronger.

35
00:03:21,521 --> 00:03:24,447
Let us clear up one big misunderstanding here.

36
00:03:24,897 --> 00:03:29,416
People say: price went up because there were more buyers.

37
00:03:29,866 --> 00:03:37,259
But every trade has one buyer and one seller. The amount bought and sold is always equal.

38
00:03:37,709 --> 00:03:43,977
The difference is who was in a hurry. Who crossed the spread and accepted the other side's price.

39
00:03:44,428 --> 00:03:51,429
Price went up because buyers were more aggressive. Not more buyers, more aggressive buyers.

40
00:03:52,429 --> 00:03:59,273
Now today's most useful idea: effort versus result.

41
00:03:59,722 --> 00:04:04,974
Effort means how hard the push was. That is, how much aggressive volume came in.

42
00:04:05,424 --> 00:04:10,492
Result means the outcome. That is, how far price actually moved.

43
00:04:10,942 --> 00:04:15,382
Look at both together, and you see the market's real story.

44
00:04:16,382 --> 00:04:18,107
Three situations are possible.

45
00:04:18,557 --> 00:04:26,916
First: big effort, big result. This is a healthy move. What you see is what is happening.

46
00:04:27,366 --> 00:04:35,333
Second: small effort, but a big result. It means there was nothing in the way. The book was thin.

47
00:04:35,783 --> 00:04:42,392
Third: very big effort, but a small result. This is the most important signal.

48
00:04:42,842 --> 00:04:48,641
It means a large passive player is sitting on the other side, absorbing all the effort.

49
00:04:49,091 --> 00:04:54,499
Price can reverse from such a spot, because those who pushed get trapped.

50
00:04:55,499 --> 00:05:01,219
Now recall the opening picture. Very big volume, but price did not move.

51
00:05:01,669 --> 00:05:09,480
That was the third situation. Aggressive buyers pushed hard, but a large passive seller kept supplying all of it.

52
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:14,684
When the buyers ran out of push, nobody was left to take price higher.

53
00:05:15,134 --> 00:05:20,463
And the buyers trapped at the top had to exit. That is why price fell.

54
00:05:21,463 --> 00:05:27,994
A common question: so is passive always smart, and aggressive always wrong?

55
00:05:28,444 --> 00:05:32,153
No. Big players use both ways.

56
00:05:32,603 --> 00:05:36,966
When they need to fill a large size quietly, they go passive.

57
00:05:37,416 --> 00:05:43,685
And when there is urgency, like around news or a breakout, the same players turn aggressive.

58
00:05:44,135 --> 00:05:50,822
Remember, a stop order also becomes a market order once triggered. So it is aggressive too.

59
00:05:51,822 --> 00:05:55,950
Now the question is, how do you see all this on a chart?

60
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:00,684
On a plain candle chart, aggressive and passive are not shown separately.

61
00:06:01,134 --> 00:06:08,370
There are special tools for this: footprint, delta and CVD. We will learn them in Module 3.

62
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:15,429
For now, just change how you think. At every candle ask: who pushed, and what was the result?

63
00:06:16,429 --> 00:06:21,261
That completes Module 1. Time for a short quiz.

64
00:06:21,711 --> 00:06:31,194
Question 1. Price broke above the range on very big volume, but the candle was small and price came back inside. What happened?

65
00:06:31,644 --> 00:06:41,440
Answer: passive sellers absorbed the aggressive buyers. Big effort, small result. And the market rejected the higher price.

66
00:06:41,890 --> 00:06:47,140
Question 2. Why does the spread widen right before big news?

67
00:06:47,590 --> 00:06:56,185
Answer: passive traders pull their limit orders. The book gets thin, so Bid and Ask move apart.

68
00:06:56,635 --> 00:07:00,344
Got both right? Then you have Module 1 down.

69
00:07:01,344 --> 00:07:02,911
So, today's summary.

70
00:07:03,361 --> 00:07:10,597
The aggressive trader takes with market orders. The passive trader places with limit orders.

71
00:07:11,047 --> 00:07:17,735
Aggressive pushes price, passive stops it. Price is the result of their fight.

72
00:07:18,185 --> 00:07:25,264
Watch effort and result together. Big effort with a small result means someone is absorbing on the other side.

73
00:07:26,264 --> 00:07:35,903
Module 2 starts next episode: the volume tools. First topic, Volume vs Tick Volume: the truth about forex and gold.

74
00:07:36,353 --> 00:07:41,186
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

75
00:07:41,636 --> 00:07:47,748
For trade setups, our Telegram and Instagram are in the description. See you in the next episode.

